    {
      "title": "Rioters loot stores, set fires in Italy during cost-of-living protest",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/rioters-loot-stores-set-fires-in-italy-during-cost-of-living-protest-126101800030_1.html",
      "description": "Rioters clashed with police, looted stores and set property on fire in central Milan on Saturday, emptying Piazza del Duomo and a luxury shopping arcade when the area would usually be teeming with shoppers.\nThe march against wars and rising living costs began peacefully. But masked protesters threw stones, paving blocks and other objects at riot police and set fire to barricades made from bar tables. Images of the aftermath showed a severely damaged carabinieri car.\nPolice used water cannons and tear gas to disperse the crowds.\nAt least two police officers were injured and about 50 demonstrators, including many minors, were detained, the LaPresse news agency reported.\nItalian Premier Giorgia Meloni condemned the violence in a social media post.\n\"Demonstrating is a right. Setting our cities ablaze is not,\" she wrote, citing \"hooligans who exploit demonstrations to sow chaos, attack those responsible for ensuring security and hold entire cities hostage\".\nMilan's centre-left mayor,",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-18T02:17:08"
    }
  ]
